#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124219 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "595fd527-e8fd-5360-87f1-acbeab511566" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Given a Linear Connection Between Two Bases"}] :question/id #uuid "4c1e01c1-d607-5ad8-8643-b3317201ab9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4832" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2\\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\{\\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2\\}$ be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ If $\\mathbf{c}_1=\\mathbf{b}_2-4\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2=5\\mathbf{b}_1+7\\mathbf{b}_2,$ find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $\\mathcal{C}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/e62970e0d84f224be492efe709d4df42872cbcc9e68c136e2f73b0e36e18f5c4.png)\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{C}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/5f5ad323ae8875717d552f068c827295954ecdb2c802f5fb3899c18884aa1f1f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124220 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b973d79-8fe4-5033-8202-9693004855dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix When One of the Bases is the Standard Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "93b466b2-9b8d-55fa-87b8-b5bf24a497cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-4833" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and let $\\mathcal{S}$ be the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ Find the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/827f1a79a51ea88e59f5d766ee531cacf3945cc2ce649521c7b1d8e7eea35e06.png)\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/82d0eab6a51dbf1615a3e2bb4fc6ffef622a38ac3c980dc742b552946c11a87a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consists of the vectors $\\mathbf e_1, \\mathbf e_2,$ and $\\mathbf e_3,$ given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/0d9a23830f48d4659dd963110d9322c12e8d4849bd9bcd63ae9d5adb0e1fba05.png)\n\nThe components of the vectors in $\\mathcal{B}$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/10b5f2e35da56c7be6d547827164c68f475df4d012116b9666c12a94bbc39f55.png)\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{S}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/06ef110e61fcdf044e15300d50bc5ac918b37e8351c1aa6b77124dd2029c9aa3.png)\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=0,$ and $c=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124221 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "23b6a85e-e1de-590c-84d2-379052b1b9f3" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Invertibility Property of the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "6c5b5534-2082-593e-af07-df83497ea84c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4835" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/07a8e394234737d51a0064816b255569d20e826eb3a82884a24f1fc0a9fa6baa.png)\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot 5-(-6)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2} & -3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124222 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f989cbaf-2d99-5267-8864-48a48db2d535" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Between Two Bases"}] :question/id #uuid "0929855b-0310-5c87-8cff-cc2faa8a5ae0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4837" :question/problem "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/51b47f81b941fffa62d1f8402a4ef08b9b7d1636b1a37a86578311f1db192b3d.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/a9964c0672c8859f4b4857d422e25a435ed0be907c074a22b7cc12729f051613.png) be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\},$ respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nSo, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/60eb4bbd057feab1b78fb283e951dc8c35f9a95875bd46f078c87c25b3b749c8.png)\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & 22 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}= & [\\begin{aligned}\\,4 & 22 \\\\ \\,1 & 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b973d79-8fe4-5033-8202-9693004855dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix When One of the Bases is the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00441517-6673-58b4-bf45-8b7dca6cff6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350907} {:answer/id #uuid "a97b3cf7-0570-5df3-8542-d1d2fa701ff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350908} {:answer/id #uuid "4b44fec3-903d-51b6-9963-7a0c4c18b01e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350909} {:answer/id #uuid "44866c73-d6b8-5585-a3b7-9fc4a4160cd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350910} {:answer/id #uuid "1d891238-2bc2-5e34-b253-68ecdfa47906" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b44fec3-903d-51b6-9963-7a0c4c18b01e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350909} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab3d048d-87bd-5c6a-8a00-67bd3bc57aa1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21801958-d4d0-51fc-9b99-d500faf26772" :question/math-academy-id "q-113115" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to S}$ from $B$ to $S$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors of $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "23b6a85e-e1de-590c-84d2-379052b1b9f3" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Invertibility Property of the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006ade43-0852-5f9e-9906-73e2f3d53e8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -9 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350914} {:answer/id #uuid "c12301fa-0178-55b3-983e-2bffb3ea7316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350915} {:answer/id #uuid "002c2d0b-7c98-521d-9f14-0d8d0c3f4dc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & -7 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350916} {:answer/id #uuid "5326255a-2783-5ec6-b6a8-14279d433f02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350917} {:answer/id #uuid "96185eaf-49c7-54df-8f84-9de85bac379b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c12301fa-0178-55b3-983e-2bffb3ea7316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350915} :answer-field/id #uuid "58f5908c-006f-5227-a07a-a0afc2203faf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84578987-2050-53d3-977c-349e71448dce" :question/math-academy-id "q-113413" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $C$ to the basis $B$ is given by\n${P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$  ." :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C} & ={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 9}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "23b6a85e-e1de-590c-84d2-379052b1b9f3" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Invertibility Property of the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cce9b22f-175a-57f1-8af8-ee78aaf3aa90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350921} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6483c3-e0e8-592f-a34e-191d53848575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350922} {:answer/id #uuid "e8cb5716-4a78-51d2-beb3-8bb19d997f5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350923} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c17bfa-436b-5b8b-8793-fa177772d04b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350924} {:answer/id #uuid "fe73a328-ba75-50af-bfc7-d2a1c316554b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cce9b22f-175a-57f1-8af8-ee78aaf3aa90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350921} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ec966db-c745-58d3-8168-70967cb0a812" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e492abb-150e-5b29-ab40-6db339f73d6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-113415" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ is given by \n${P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$  ." :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f989cbaf-2d99-5267-8864-48a48db2d535" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "023fb4ae-95dd-5232-b2fa-bdb063de7ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350928} {:answer/id #uuid "21293c50-75f5-590e-b4d1-65b7bf3084f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350929} {:answer/id #uuid "8e46a25b-451b-5cf2-ae82-50a32d7753a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350930} {:answer/id #uuid "3767a592-f2a6-5e7a-ad9e-ad21d6e80d68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350931} {:answer/id #uuid "23b65d6f-72ee-5e43-86f7-60521a53ab1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3767a592-f2a6-5e7a-ad9e-ad21d6e80d68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350931} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae3ab330-db7d-538e-a512-6c80e70424f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5526d0bc-df0c-5412-80a7-6cc565ad8832" :question/math-academy-id "q-113510" :question/problem "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}$ and $C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 9 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f989cbaf-2d99-5267-8864-48a48db2d535" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d63c9e0-2100-5e1c-ae84-cda3ae6601b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350935} {:answer/id #uuid "325ac13f-4566-5a7e-bf93-25f023d35026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350936} {:answer/id #uuid "60aba3c2-ccba-5ae9-a35d-4923acbae8a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350937} {:answer/id #uuid "8eda1e88-507e-5eec-b51e-08f547560e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350938} {:answer/id #uuid "dc90ddc8-546b-5db8-afc2-7e42f793937e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "325ac13f-4566-5a7e-bf93-25f023d35026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350936} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a63866f-8e2e-55dc-8442-0a2a5477215f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4593a4d9-b03b-5c1f-ad85-95d87fe0ce9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-113513" :question/problem "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $S$ is the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f989cbaf-2d99-5267-8864-48a48db2d535" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22fc78bb-6bc1-551d-85fa-79b71640f31f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350942} {:answer/id #uuid "37656f9d-4cfa-5ffd-8809-e34784c3f22c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & \\frac{1}{17}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350943} {:answer/id #uuid "bc95c339-47e2-5e1c-9edf-6ea25645f0ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350944} {:answer/id #uuid "44d21186-f6d2-545e-81fd-6eefd87f7f57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350945} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ccb825-9eef-53f4-88ca-2646956a5d90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8ccb825-9eef-53f4-88ca-2646956a5d90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350946} :answer-field/id #uuid "574e9abf-ae04-5396-b5b5-53a11c296b27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c98acfad-2f66-5567-b9e7-daf3db02ed5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-113515" :question/problem "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{17}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{4}{17} & \\frac{1}{17} & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{4}{17} & \\frac{1}{17} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{17} & -\\frac{4}{17} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=17{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{4}{17} & \\frac{1}{17} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{4}{17}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 17\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f989cbaf-2d99-5267-8864-48a48db2d535" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Between Two Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af26bf47-93e7-5bc5-9456-0448647b39be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350949} {:answer/id #uuid "e097af62-101a-5dae-8610-6353119b1ff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350950} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca23ca6-bb6d-561a-8da2-a041611dea09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350951} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb8f6e1-5439-5cc2-a2ed-687be3b8e063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350952} {:answer/id #uuid "f183fa21-594a-57a0-b5b5-7f8b6105a02a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcb8f6e1-5439-5cc2-a2ed-687be3b8e063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350952} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7bcaad0-35c3-59ba-bf45-21872573d9de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :q